From 1af13371778901d034e013934729cdf57de49ed8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 20:58:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10190/11362] exp5879: unrelated suite debt no longer blocks a
 task whose science is done

This module already did the hard part. `classify_test_debt` separated its own
checks from the global suite, correctly labelled this case
`unrelated_global_suite_debt`, and then blocked on it anyway --
`blocks_terminal_ready_status = unrelated_global_suite_debt or owned_failed` --
so the classification had no consequence. Its verdict has read
"blocked: science_ready_but_unrelated_global_suite_debt" while its own science
score was 1.0: the module saying its work is done and something else is in the
way, every run, with no route out.

Now it blocks only when the unrelated debt is a REGRESSION, decided by the
REQ-HARNESS-5920 node-id delta against ops/global_suite_failure_baseline.json.
The suite still runs in full and every failure stays visible; nothing is
suppressed, deselected or relabelled.

Fails closed, in two ways that matter. No node-id evidence recorded means the
delta cannot be computed, so the debt blocks exactly as before -- an unmeasured
failure must never read as a clean delta, which is a bug I wrote into exp6682
earlier today and its tests caught. And an OWNED check failing still blocks
unconditionally; this changes nothing about the module's own verification.

Verified on the real baseline: baseline debt only -> not blocking; one new node
id -> blocking; no evidence -> blocking; an owned check failing -> blocking.
Focused suite 6/6.

SCOPE, deliberately one module and not ten. A survey of the other nine showed
none is actually blocked by unrelated debt: five are already complete or ready,
and four are blocked by their own science -- a missing GPU-offload receipt, a
failed model-fold cell, failed infrastructure checks, an archived trace that
lacked what it needed. Wiring the delta into those would change no outcome
while touching nine artifact-writing modules with nine different readiness
shapes. The regression-detection argument for doing them anyway is real but
speculative, and applies to the whole repo rather than to nine hand-picked
files, so it belongs to a repo-wide mechanism.

